var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"guides/solvers_gpu/#Linear-solvers-and-GPU","page":"Linear solvers and GPU","text":"Almost all of the time in a simulation goes into Newton's linear systems. On the CPU they are solved with a sparse direct method. On an NVIDIA GPU the whole Newton iteration can run on the device: residual and Jacobian kernels, sparse factorization and solve.","title":"Linear solvers and GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/solvers_gpu/#Choosing-the-sparse-direct-solver","page":"Linear solvers and GPU","text":"Every Newton solve goes through the solver passed as linear_solver:\n\nbackend where requires\nKLUBackend() CPU (default) nothing\nSparseDirectSolverBackend() CPU and GPU using SparseDirectSolver\nCUDSSBackend() GPU using CUDA, CUDSS\n\nusing GradPower, SparseDirectSolver\nt, traj = integrate!(dp, sys, 10.0; linear_solver = SparseDirectSolverBackend())\n\nlinear_solver works with every solver= choice and with integrate_batched!. SparseDirectSolver.jl is not registered. Install it with Pkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/exanauts/SparseDirectSolver.jl\").","title":"Choosing the sparse direct solver"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/solvers_gpu/#Running-on-the-GPU","page":"Linear solvers and GPU","text":"Loading CUDA and a GPU solver activates GradPower's CUDA extension. A batch of M scenarios is copied to the device once, integrated there, and the trajectories come back at the end:\n\nusing GradPower, CUDA, CUDSS\next = Base.get_extension(GradPower, :GradPowerCUDAExt)\n\nsys = from_psse(\"examples/ieee9_v33.raw\", \"examples/ieee9bus_gov.dyr\")\nbuild_network!(sys); runpf!(sys)\ndp = DynamicProblem(sys); initialize_dynamics!(dp, sys)\nadd_event!(sys, ContingencyEvent(sys.busmap[7], 0.02, 0.1, 0.2))\n\ngbl = ext.GpuBatchedLayout(dp, sys, 16)                # 16 scenarios on the device\nt, trajs = ext.integrate_gpu_cudss!(gbl, sys, 1.0)     # monolithic Newton\nt, trajs = ext.integrate_gpu_schur_cudss!(gbl, sys, 1.0) # Schur-reduced Newton\n\nWithout linear_solver, GpuBatchedLayout chooses the solver from the batch size:\n\nbelow 128 scenarios: cuDSS;\nfrom 128 scenarios: SparseDirectSolver, if it is loaded.\n\nSparseDirectSolver's uniform batch keeps getting cheaper per system up to about 1,000 systems. cuDSS has to be split into chunks of 15, and its cost per system is flat beyond that. On ACTIVSg2000 the full GPU Schur integration ties at 64 scenarios, and SparseDirectSolver is 1.3× faster at 256.\n\nLike BatchedLayout, GpuBatchedLayout holds per-scenario states and parameters (gbl.z, gbl.p). Scenarios with different fault buses or impedances share one factorization through low-rank updates:\n\nt, trajs = ext.integrate_gpu_shared_multi!(gbl, sys, 1.0,\n                fault_buses, rfaults, 0.1, 0.2)   # one (bus, r) per scenario","title":"Running on the GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/solvers_gpu/#Storing-less","page":"Linear solvers and GPU","text":"The GPU integrators take the same output options as integrate!: save_every, save_idxs and save_eltype. Only the selected rows of the selected steps are kept on the device, and they are downloaded once at the end:\n\nnet  = sys.dynamic.diff_dim + sys.dynamic.alg_dim\nrows = collect(net + 1 : net + 2length(sys.buses))        # bus voltages only\nt, V = ext.integrate_gpu_schur_cudss!(gbl, sys, 1.0;\n           save_idxs = rows, save_every = 2, save_eltype = Float32)\n\nOn ACTIVSg2000 (1 s, 256 scenarios) this takes 3.4 s instead of 6.6 s for the full trajectory. Device memory drops from 5.3 to 3.7 GiB, and the output from 1.9 GB to 0.29 GB.\n\nThe GPU integrators support resistive bus faults (ContingencyEvent without xfault). If other events are registered (trips, load steps, impedance faults), they raise an ArgumentError rather than silently ignoring them; use the CPU integrators for those.","title":"Storing less"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/solvers_gpu/#When-is-the-GPU-faster?","page":"Linear solvers and GPU","text":"Measured on one Quadro GV100 and a 4× Xeon Gold 6140 server, 1 s with a bus fault, Jacobian reuse on:\n\ncase (unknowns) scenarios GPU CPU (threads)\nACTIVSg2000 (7.5 k) 1 0.15 s 0.14 s\nACTIVSg2000 16 0.84 s 1.5 s\nACTIVSg2000 64 3.5 s 2.6 s (64)\nACTIVSg70k (217 k) 1 0.94 s 9.3 s\nACTIVSg70k 16 18 s 54 s (16)\nACTIVSg70k 64 93 s 162 s (64)\n\nAs a rule of thumb:\n\nLarge networks benefit most: one 70 k-bus simulation is about 10× faster.\nSmall and medium networks in big batches usually run as fast or faster on a many-core CPU with integrate_batched!.\nGPU memory grows with the batch, about 0.33 GiB per ACTIVSg70k scenario.","title":"When is the GPU faster?"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/solvers_gpu/#Schur-reduction","page":"Linear solvers and GPU","text":"With solver = :schur (CPU) or integrate_gpu_schur_cudss! (GPU), each Newton system is solved by first eliminating every device cluster. A cluster is a machine with its controllers, and its block is small and dense. What remains is the network-sized system, which has the same sparsity as the admittance matrix. The dense blocks are independent, so they batch well on the GPU. See How it works for the algebra.","title":"Schur reduction"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/datasets/#Generating-training-data","page":"Generating training data","text":"GradPower ships a pipeline that generates transient-stability datasets: many fault simulations over many operating points, with a stability label for each, written to HDF5. It lives in scripts/:\n\nfile role\nscripts/generate_dynamics.jl the driver: reads a TOML sweep, shards across MPI/SLURM ranks, writes one HDF5 per rank\nscripts/dynstab_io.jl the building blocks: case loading, load scaling, simulation, labels, HDF5 layout\nscripts/sweeps/*.toml sweep definitions\nscripts/tutorial_generate_data.jl a commented single-process walkthrough\n\njulia --project=scripts -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'   # once\njulia --project=scripts scripts/generate_dynamics.jl scripts/sweeps/example.toml --dry-run\njulia --project=scripts scripts/generate_dynamics.jl scripts/sweeps/example.toml","title":"Generating training data"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/datasets/#One-sample","page":"Generating training data","text":"A sample is one simulation: an operating point (load scale λ), a fault (bus, resistance, duration) and the response. By default simulate_sample records all state rows in Float64: device differential and algebraic states, plus real and imaginary voltage at every bus. downsample affects only the saved time columns. The output includes actual saved times and a row schema with external bus, model, device ID, state name, kind, and initial online flag.\n\nUse output_mode=:derived_channels explicitly for only bus voltage magnitude and angle and machine rotor angle and speed in Float32. The default is output_mode=:all_states, including when no option is supplied.\n\nThe labels are accumulated from every step by a step callback.\n\nusing GradPower, CairoMakie\ninclude(joinpath(pkgdir(GradPower), \"scripts\", \"dynstab_io.jl\"))\n\ncase = Dict(\"raw\" => \"examples/IEEE39.raw\", \"dyr\" => \"examples/IEEE39_gov.dyr\")\nsys, base = build_system(case)\ndprob, residual = initialized_problem(sys)\nz0 = copy(dprob.zvec)\n\nfunction sample(bus, r, duration; kw...)\n    empty!(sys.dynamic.events)\n    GradPower.add_event!(sys, ContingencyEvent(sys.busmap[bus], r, 0.2, 0.2 + duration))\n    dprob.zvec .= z0\n    simulate_sample(dprob, sys, 5.0; dt = 1/120, downsample = 2, kw...)\nend\n\nch, m = sample(16, 0.005, 0.1)\n(keys(ch)..., ), size(ch[\"states\"]), m[\"stable\"], round(m[\"max_angle_sep_deg\"], digits = 1)\n((\"states\", \"time\", \"output_mode\", \"state_schema\", \"T\"), (238, 301), true, 24.2)","title":"One sample"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/datasets/#Stable-or-not?","page":"Generating training data","text":"The label is stable = true when both hold:\n\nthe rotor angles never separate by more than 180° relative to the pre-fault state;\nthe swing has decayed to at most half its peak by the end of the window.\n\nSweeping the fault duration at one bus crosses the critical clearing time:\n\ndurations = [0.10, 0.20, 0.30, 0.35, 0.40, 0.50]\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 360))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"time (s)\", ylabel = \"max rotor angle separation (deg)\",\n          yscale = Makie.pseudolog10, yticks = [0, 10, 100, 1000, 10000],\n          title = \"fault at bus 16, r = 0.005 pu\")\nfor d in durations\n    ch, m = sample(16, 0.005, d; output_mode=:derived_channels)\n    delta = ch[\"gen_delta\"]\n    sep = vec(maximum(delta .- delta[:, 1], dims = 1) .- minimum(delta .- delta[:, 1], dims = 1))\n    t = ch[\"time\"]\n    lines!(ax, t, rad2deg.(sep), label = \"$(Int(1000d)) ms → \" * (m[\"stable\"] ? \"stable\" : \"UNSTABLE\"),\n           linestyle = m[\"stable\"] ? :solid : :dash)\nend\naxislegend(ax, position = :lt)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nUp to 300 ms the machines swing a few tens of degrees and settle. From 350 ms they separate without bound, because a machine slips poles and nothing trips it. The critical clearing time of this fault lies between the two. The gap between the two populations is huge, and that is why the label is robust.","title":"Stable or not?"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/datasets/#A-small-sweep","page":"Generating training data","text":"The same loop over buses and durations gives a picture of the case's stability boundary. With early_stop_sep, unstable samples are cut as soon as they pass 360°, which saves most of their cost without changing any label:\n\nbuses = [2, 4, 8, 16, 21, 26, 29]\ngrid = falses(length(buses), length(durations))\ntstop = zeros(length(buses), length(durations))\nfor (i, b) in enumerate(buses), (j, d) in enumerate(durations)\n    _, m = sample(b, 0.005, d; early_stop_sep = deg2rad(360))\n    grid[i, j] = m[\"stable\"]; tstop[i, j] = m[\"t_stop\"]\nend\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"fault duration (ms)\", ylabel = \"faulted bus\",\n          xticks = (1:length(durations), string.(Int.(1000 .* durations))),\n          yticks = (1:length(buses), string.(buses)),\n          title = \"r = 0.005 pu: stable (green) / unstable (red); number = simulated seconds\")\nheatmap!(ax, 1:length(durations), 1:length(buses), Float64.(permutedims(grid)),\n         colormap = [:tomato, :mediumseagreen], colorrange = (0, 1))\nfor i in eachindex(buses), j in eachindex(durations)\n    text!(ax, j, i, text = string(round(tstop[i, j], digits = 1)), align = (:center, :center))\nend\nfig\nFigure()\n\nEvery unstable cell stopped 1–2 s into the run instead of simulating the full 5 s. The boundary moves with the location: a fault at bus 2 can last 100 ms longer than one at bus 16.","title":"A small sweep"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/datasets/#The-sweep-file","page":"Generating training data","text":"A sweep is the cross product of loading draws × fault buses × resistances × durations:\n\noutput_root = \"./dynstab_out\"\n\n[sim]\nt_final    = 10.0\ndt         = 0.008333333333333333   # 1/120 s\ndownsample = 2                      # store every 2nd step\noutput_mode = \"all_states\"          # default, even when omitted\n# output_mode = \"derived_channels\" # explicit compact Float32 output\nearly_stop_deg = 360.0              # optional: stop unstable runs early\n\n[load]\ndistribution = \"uniform\"            # lambda ~ U(low, high)\nlow = 0.95\nhigh = 1.05\nn_samples = 10                      # operating points\nseed = 20240917\n\n[fault]\nbuses     = \"all\"                   # or a list of PSS/E bus numbers\nr_fault   = [0.01, 0.05, 0.2]\nt_on      = 0.2\ndurations = [0.10, 0.20, 0.25, 0.30, 0.35]\n\n[label]\nangle_sep_threshold_deg = 180.0\ntail_fraction           = 0.2\ndecay_ratio             = 0.5\n\n[[cases]]\nname = \"ieee39\"\nraw  = \"examples/IEEE39.raw\"\ndyr  = \"examples/IEEE39_gov.dyr\"\n\nFor each λ draw, the power flow and initialization run once and are shared by all the faults below it. Before using an operating point, the driver also checks that it is a stable equilibrium (check_self_stability).","title":"The sweep file"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/datasets/#HDF5-layout","page":"Generating training data","text":"<case>_rank0000.h5\n├── attributes: schema_version=4, output_mode, case_name, baseMVA, dt, dt_eff, t_final, downsample, ...\n├── grid/            topology, once per file: bus_id, bus_type, branch_index, gen_bus, ...\n├── state_schema/    index, model, bus, device_id, name, kind, online (all_states only)\n└── samples/\n    ├── 000001/      time [T], states [length(z) × T] (Float64, all_states default)\n    │                attributes: load_scale, fault_bus_ext, r_fault, t_on, t_off, clearing_time,\n    │                            stable, max_angle_sep_deg, max_freq_dev, tail_peak_ratio, ...\n    │                            (+ terminated_early, t_stop, stop_reason with early stop)\n    └── ...\n\n/grid holds what a graph neural network needs: the nodes are buses, the edges are branch_index, and machines are attached through gen_bus.\n\nIn derived_channels mode, each sample contains bus_vm, bus_va, gen_delta, and gen_omega instead of states, and no /state_schema is written. Both modes store time[T]; use it rather than reconstructing times from the requested horizon, particularly with early stopping.\n\nSchema entries describe rows in recording order. Use (model, bus, device_id, name) to identify a raw state across layouts, not the solver row index. See scripts/README_datagen.md for a readback example.","title":"HDF5 layout"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/datasets/#Pitfalls","page":"Generating training data","text":"These are documented in detail in scripts/README_datagen.md. The most important:\n\nThe horizon must resolve the first swing. Large systems peak late; use 10 s for ACTIVSg2000.\nClearing times must straddle the critical clearing time, or every sample lands in one class.\nFault severity is not monotonic in r. A resistive fault absorbs the most power when r matches the local Thevenin impedance, not as r → 0.","title":"Pitfalls"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Building-a-system","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Building a system"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Queries-and-inspection","page":"API reference","text":"See Queries and parameter edits for identity conventions and an IEEE9 example. Bus selectors default to external numbering; internal indices require numbering=:internal.","title":"Queries and inspection"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Parameter-and-operating-point-edits","page":"API reference","text":"Parameter edits are CPU-only and between runs, never during integration. Operating-point edits require power flow followed by a new dynamic problem and initialization; existing problems and GPU/batch layouts must be recreated.","title":"Parameter and operating-point edits"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Dynamic-problem","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Dynamic problem"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Events","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Events"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Solvers-and-diagnostics","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Solvers and diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Optional-NLP-Model","page":"API reference","text":"Install and load NLPModels and MadNLP before constructing the model. The constructor returns an NLPModels.AbstractNLPModel implemented in GradPowerNLPExt; GradPower.DynamicNLP itself is a function, not a type. Only forward objective evaluation is supported. Gradients still raise an explicit error, so gradient-based optimization is not currently available.","title":"Optional NLP Model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.from_psse","page":"API reference","text":"from_psse(raw_data_file::String, dyr_file::String)\n\nReads a PSSE raw file and a PSSE dyr file and constructs a PowerSystem\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.from_psse"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.build_network!","page":"API reference","text":"build_network!(sys)\n\nBuild the network admittance matrix of sys (complex Y and its realified form used by the dynamics) from its branches and shunts. Call it after reading or modifying the network and before runpf!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.build_network!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.runpf!","page":"API reference","text":"runpf!(psys; verbose=false, fdiff=false, enforce_q_limits=true,\n       max_q_outer=50, q_tol=1e-6)\n\nSame as runpf but writes the solution back into the PowerSystem in place.\n\nGenerator reactive limits (enforce_q_limits)\n\nWith enforce_q_limits=false (the default before v0.3) a PV bus holds its scheduled voltage no matter how much reactive power that takes. The solution is then a fixed point of the power-flow equations but not necessarily a physical operating point: machines can be left absorbing or producing far outside their nameplate QT/QB.\n\nThat is not a cosmetic problem. On ACTIVSg2000, 200 of 432 generators solve outside their limits (one bus absorbs 279 MVAr against an 8 MVAr floor). The excess reactive flow drags 20 machines past their pull-out angle – internal angle delta - angle(V) above 90 degrees, where dP/ddelta < 0 – which puts 18 non-oscillatory unstable eigenvalues into the linearised dynamics. Any transient run from that point diverges regardless of the disturbance. On ACTIVSg70k, 470 machines start above 150 degrees and one at 179.5 degrees slips a pole after a fault; its unstable mode amplifies solver round-off by about 2x per step, so trajectories depend on the Newton tolerance.\n\nWith enforce_q_limits=true (the default, and uqgrid's default)  we run the standard outer loop:\n\nSolve the power flow with the current PV/PQ assignment.\nFor each bus that started as PV, compare the reactive dispatch against the sum of its generators' limits. If it is above the aggregate qmax (below qmin), convert the bus to PQ, hold every generator there at its own qmax (qmin), and release the voltage.\nRepeat until an iteration converts nothing, or max_q_outer is reached.\n\nPinning each generator at its own limit is exact rather than a heuristic: a bus sits at its aggregate limit precisely when every machine on it is at its own.\n\nConversion is one-way, as in MATPOWER's default enforce_q_lims = 1. Allowing a pinned bus back to PV once its voltage recovers past setpoint is tempting and is what enforce_q_lims = 2 does, but on ACTIVSg2000 it cycles: a handful of buses pin, release, and re-pin forever, and the loop exits on the iteration cap with those buses still violating. One-way conversion is monotone – every iteration either pins at least one more bus or stops – so it terminates, and when it stops no PV bus is outside its limits. The cost is mild conservatism: a bus can stay pinned when it could have held its voltage after the rest of the system moved.\n\nThe slack bus is never limited – it has to absorb the system mismatch, and capping it would leave the power flow with no degree of freedom to close on. If the slack ends up outside its limits that is a property of the case, and verbose=true reports it.\n\nBuses whose only generation is a StaticGenerator stub are switched here too, but note the stub regulates voltage with unlimited reactive power during the transient regardless – the limit is respected by the operating point, not by the dynamics.\n\nLimits come from the .raw generator records (QT/QB, converted to the system base at parse time). Records that carry the usual +/-9999 placeholder simply never bind. Cases built without limit data default to +/-Inf, so enforce_q_limits=true is a no-op for them rather than an error.\n\nPinning every violator at once can make the re-solve diverge: on ACTIVSg2000 at perturbed dispatches several hundred buses violate together. When that happens the loop backs off and pins only the worst half of them, and so on down to one. If even that one does not converge, or the initial solve does not, runpf! throws a PowerFlowError and the system keeps its last converged solution. (It used to write the diverged voltages back as if solved.)\n\nReturns the number of buses left pinned at a limit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.runpf!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.runpf","page":"API reference","text":"runpf(sys; verbose=false) -> (v, success)\n\nNewton power flow on sys without writing the solution back and without reactive limits. Most users want runpf!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.runpf"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.analyze_dyr_coverage","page":"API reference","text":"analyze_dyr_coverage(raw_path, dyr_path; redirects=Dict())\n\nClassify every DYR record as exactly one of :native, :redirected, :unsupported, :unmatched, :inactive, or :duplicate. Static equipment is matched using the external RAW bus number and normalized PSS/E ID. Redirects are opt-in and never contribute to native coverage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.analyze_dyr_coverage"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.bus_index","page":"API reference","text":"bus_index(ps, number; numbering=:external)\n\nResolve an external bus number or explicit internal index to an internal index. Missing buses and invalid numbering modes raise ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.bus_index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.get_bus","page":"API reference","text":"get_bus(ps, number; numbering=:external) -> Bus\n\nReturn the bus selected by external number or explicit internal index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.get_bus"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.find_generator","page":"API reference","text":"find_generator(ps, bus, id; numbering=:external)\n\nReturn the unique static generator row index, comparing normalized PSS/E IDs. Missing or ambiguous identities raise ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.find_generator"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.find_load","page":"API reference","text":"find_load(ps, bus, id; numbering=:external)\n\nReturn the unique static load row index, comparing normalized PSS/E IDs. Missing or ambiguous identities raise ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.find_load"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.find_devices","page":"API reference","text":"find_devices(ps; model=nothing, bus=nothing, id=nothing,\n             numbering=:external, online=nothing)\n\nReturn dynamic device indices matching all supplied filters. Models are case-insensitive names (including :gensal and :static_gen aliases) or device types. IDs follow state_channels, including static-generator aggregate IDs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.find_devices"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.find_device","page":"API reference","text":"find_device(ps, model, bus, id; numbering=:external)\n\nReturn a unique dynamic device index; missing or ambiguous matches are errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.find_device"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.state_index","page":"API reference","text":"state_index(ps, model, bus, id, name; numbering=:external)\n\nReturn the unique current StateChannel.index. State names are case-sensitive; network voltages use model :bus, an empty ID, and :vr or :vi.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.state_index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.parameter_index","page":"API reference","text":"parameter_index(ps, device_index, name)\nparameter_index(ps, model, bus, id, name; numbering=:external)\n\nReturn the current parameter-vector index. Parameter names are case-sensitive Symbols or strings; missing or ambiguous names raise ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.parameter_index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.system_summary","page":"API reference","text":"system_summary(ps; section=:buses) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nReturn rows for :buses, :generators, :loads, or dynamic :devices. Rows contain index, external bus, and id plus section-specific data. Device IDs are normalized; bus names are preserved. Device rows include model, online status, and current vector pointers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.system_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.display_parameters","page":"API reference","text":"display_parameters([io], ps)\n\nPrint device parameter names and their current vector indices to io (stdout).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.display_parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.get_bus_info","page":"API reference","text":"get_bus_info([io], bus, ps)\n\nPrint a bus and its connected static rows, branches, shunts, and dynamic devices. All displayed bus numbers are external; output defaults to stdout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.get_bus_info"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.get_index_info","page":"API reference","text":"get_index_info(idx, ps) -> StateChannel\nget_index_info(io, idx, ps) -> StateChannel\n\nReturn metadata for a current raw state index, raising BoundsError outside the state vector. The IO overload also prints the channel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.get_index_info"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.get_parameter","page":"API reference","text":"get_parameter(sys, device_index, name)\nget_parameter(dp, sys, device_index, name)\nget_parameter([dp,] sys, model, bus, id, name; numbering=:external)\n\nRead a named device parameter, using the device configuration or, when dp is supplied, its live parameter vector. Names are case-sensitive and match get_param_names. Values use the model's internal, system-base units.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.get_parameter"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.set_parameter!","page":"API reference","text":"set_parameter!(dp, sys, device_index, name, value)\nset_parameter!(dp, sys, model, bus, id, name, value; numbering=:external)\n\nEdit a native device parameter between runs. Synchronizes the device struct, dp.pvec, and its CPU table snapshot. The edit survives subsequent initialize_dynamics! calls. Names and units match get_parameter.\n\nInitialization-derived parameters (controller references, static-generator setpoints and load admittances), load powers, and wiring selectors are read-only. Use set_load! / set_generator! for operating-point changes. No state is reset: call initialize_dynamics! to obtain a new equilibrium when needed. Do not call during integration or on a GPU/batched problem. Other problems or GPU/batch layouts sharing sys must be recreated.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.set_parameter!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.set_load!","page":"API reference","text":"set_load!(sys, bus, id; pd=nothing, qd=nothing, numbering=:external)\n\nSet static load power in pu on the system base before power flow. Updates any mapped ZIPLoad configuration and table as well. Does not change topology. After editing, run runpf!, construct a new DynamicProblem, and initialize it. Existing problems and GPU/batch layouts are stale and must not be reused. Returns the edited static Load.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.set_load!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.set_generator!","page":"API reference","text":"set_generator!(sys, bus, id; psch=nothing, qsch=nothing,\n               qmin=nothing, qmax=nothing, numbering=:external)\n\nEdit a static generator schedule / reactive limits in system-base pu, before power flow. Infinite reactive limits are allowed. Bus type, status, machine base and network topology are not editable here. After editing, run runpf!, construct a new DynamicProblem, and initialize it; discard existing problems and GPU/batch layouts. Returns the edited static Gen.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.set_generator!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.DynamicProblem","page":"API reference","text":"DynamicProblem(sys)\n\nAllocate the state zvec, control uvec and parameter pvec vectors of the dynamic model of sys. Fill them with initialize_dynamics!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.DynamicProblem"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.initialize_dynamics!","page":"API reference","text":"initialize_dynamics!(dp, sys)\n\nInitialize every device at the power-flow operating point stored in sys (run runpf! first): machine internal states, controller setpoints and load admittances, so that the DAE residual is zero at dp.zvec.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.initialize_dynamics!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.integrate!","page":"API reference","text":"integrate!(dp, ps, tf; dt=1/120, solver=:monolithic, linear_solver=KLUBackend(),\n           newton_tol=1e-10, jacobian_reuse=true, predictor=true, ...) -> (tvec, traj)\n\nIntegration of the DAE from dp.zvec to tf with fixed step dt.\n\nintegrator = :backward_euler (default) or :crank_nicolson (trapezoidal rule on the differential equations, z_{k+1} = z_k + dt/2 (f(z_{k+1}) + f(z_k)), algebraic equations enforced at t_{k+1}; second order away from events). Events and their dt = 0 algebraic re-solves are the same for both; a CN step after an event starts from the re-solved state. CN is not available with limit_method != :none (the limit equations are backward-Euler).\n\njacobian_reuse=true keeps the Jacobian factorization across Newton iterations and time steps (modified Newton) and refactors only when convergence slows down, at events, or after a failed solve; see JacobianReuse. predictor=true starts each step from the linear extrapolation 2 z_k - z_{k-1} (not across events). Both converge to the same newton_tol; set both to false for full Newton from the previous state. They apply to solver=:monolithic and :schur; :schur_gmres and limit_method != :none always use full Newton.\n\nOutput\n\nBy default the full trajectory is returned: traj[:, j] is the state at tvec[j], for every step. To store less:\n\nsave_every = s stores the steps 1:s:nsteps+1 (step 1 = initial state); the final step is not added when it is not on that grid. tvec holds the stored times.\nsave_idxs stores only those rows of the state (traj[r, j] = z[save_idxs[r]]); default : (all rows).\nsave_eltype = Float32 stores in that element type.\nresult = true returns a SimulationResult with named state accessors and metadata. The default remains (tvec, traj). scenario_ids may supply a one-element vector identifying this run.\nstep_callback(k, t, z) is called after the initial state (k = 1) and after every accepted step k = 2, …, nsteps+1, with the full state z at time t, whether or not it is stored. z must not be modified.\n\nEarly termination\n\nstop_condition(k, t, z) -> Bool is evaluated after every accepted step k = 2, …, nsteps+1 (after step_callback, before that step's events). When it returns true the integration ends at t: dp.zvec is set to that state and the output holds the stored steps up to it (tvec[end] <= t; with save_every > 1 the stop step itself is stored only if it is on the grid). The caller's condition records why it stopped. z must not be modified.\n\nAt an event step the stored (and passed) state is the one before the event's algebraic re-solve. These options do not change the computed solution; dp.zvec is always set to the final state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.integrate!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.integrate_batched!","page":"API reference","text":"integrate_batched!(bl, ps, tf; dt=1/120, newton_tol=1e-10, linear_solver=KLUBackend(),\n                   jacobian_reuse=true, predictor=true, threaded=true) -> (tvec, trajs)\n\nIntegrate the bl.M scenarios of bl (each with its own initial state bl.z[m, :] and parameters bl.p[m, :]) through the events registered on ps. Every scenario runs the same Newton solve as integrate! with solver=:monolithic, so a scenario's trajectory equals integrate! with the same z, p and options. Scenarios run on Threads.nthreads() threads when threaded=true. integrator is :backward_euler (default) or :crank_nicolson, as in integrate!. Returns trajs[m], the (sys_dim, nsteps+1) trajectory of scenario m; the final states are written back to bl.z.\n\nsave_every, save_idxs and save_eltype select the stored output as in integrate! (same for every scenario). step_callback(m, k, t, z) is called for scenario m with its state after the initial state (k = 1) and after every accepted step; with threaded=true it runs on worker threads, concurrently for different m, so it must only touch per-scenario data. result=true opts into SimulationResult; scenario_ids supplies unique labels in scenario order (default 1:bl.M).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.integrate_batched!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.SimulationResult","page":"API reference","text":"SimulationResult(times, trajectories, channels; scenario_ids, status, events,\n                 metadata, storage)\nintegrate!(dp, ps, tf; result=true, ...)\n\nNamed simulation output. Integrators retain their tuple return by default; result=true opts in. trajectories is a matrix for a single simulation or a vector of matrices for batched simulations. Rows follow channels, columns follow times. Arrays are not copied. storage is an optional caller-owned storage handle; no file ownership or automatic closing is implied.\n\nstatus is one symbol per scenario (:success or :terminated for the built-in CPU integrators). Failed solves still throw and do not return partial results. events describes applied scheduled events, not merely requested events. Tuple destructuring (times, trajectories = result) is also supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.SimulationResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.StateChannel","page":"API reference","text":"StateChannel\n\nIdentity of one saved raw state row. index is its original simulation row, not a persistent identity: use (model, bus, device_id, name) across layouts. bus is an external PSS/E number, kind is :differential or :algebraic, and online records the device status at the start of the simulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.StateChannel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.state_channels","page":"API reference","text":"state_channels(ps; save_idxs=:) -> Vector{StateChannel}\n\nDescribe raw state rows in recording order, including network :bus states :vr and :vi. Duplicate/subset indices follow the recorder exactly. Static generator aggregates use a sorted, quoted list of member IDs as their device ID (a single member uses its own ID).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.state_channels"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.state","page":"API reference","text":"state(result, model, bus, device_id, name; scenario=first(result.scenario_ids))\nstate(result, model, k, name; scenario=first(result.scenario_ids))\n\nView of a raw saved state. Explicit identity uses external bus and normalized PSS/E device_id. The short form selects the kth recorded device of model, sorted by (bus, device_id), not by solver layout. Model symbols are lowercase; state names retain their declared case. Missing rows raise ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.speed","page":"API reference","text":"speed(result, k; model=:genrou, scenario=first(result.scenario_ids))\nspeed(result, bus, device_id; model=:genrou, scenario=first(result.scenario_ids))\n\nView of raw :w: per-unit speed deviation, not absolute speed or Hz.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.speed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.bus_voltage","page":"API reference","text":"bus_voltage(result, external_bus; component=:complex, scenario=first(result.scenario_ids))\n\nNetwork voltage in pu. :real and :imag return raw views; :complex, :magnitude and :angle allocate derived vectors (angle in radians). Both raw components must have been recorded for derived output.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.bus_voltage"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.BatchedLayout","page":"API reference","text":"BatchedLayout(dp, ps, M)\n\nM scenarios, each starting from dp.zvec with parameters dp.pvec. Edit bl.z[m, :] / bl.p[m, :] to make the scenarios differ, then call integrate_batched! (CPU) or build a GpuBatchedLayout from it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.BatchedLayout"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.gen_speeds","page":"API reference","text":"gen_speeds(sys) -> Vector\n\nRows of the state vector holding the rotor speed deviation of every GENROU machine (the rotor angle is the next row).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.gen_speeds"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.ContingencyEvent","page":"API reference","text":"ContingencyEvent(bus, rfault, ton, toff; xfault=0.0)\n\nShunt fault at internal bus bus, active for ton <= t < toff, with fault impedance rfault + j xfault (pu), i.e. admittance y = 1/(rfault + j xfault) to ground. xfault = 0 (default) is the resistive fault y = 1/rfault. rfault >= 0 and xfault must be finite and not both zero: a bolted fault (zero impedance) is not representable; use a small impedance instead. The GPU integrators support xfault = 0 only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.ContingencyEvent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.add_event!","page":"API reference","text":"add_event!(sys, ev::ContingencyEvent)\n\nRegister a bus fault on sys; it is applied by every subsequent integration until removed from sys.dynamic.events.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.add_event!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.TripLineEvent","page":"API reference","text":"TripLineEvent\n\nTrip a transmission line at ton by subtracting its admittance from ybus_real. If toff < Inf, the line is reclosed at toff by adding the admittance back.\n\nFields:\n\nfrom_bus, to_bus: internal bus indices\nton: time at which the line is tripped\ntoff: time at which the line is reclosed (Inf = permanent trip)\nyff, yft, ytf, ytt: precomputed complex admittance contributions\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.TripLineEvent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.create_trip_line_event","page":"API reference","text":"create_trip_line_event(ps, from_bus_ext, to_bus_ext, ton; toff=Inf, ckt=nothing) -> TripLineEvent\n\nCreate a TripLineEvent (line removal) by looking up the branch between from_bus_ext and to_bus_ext (external PSS/E bus numbers, circuit ckt; see find_branch) and precomputing its admittance contribution. The line is tripped at ton and reclosed at toff (default Inf = permanent).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.create_trip_line_event"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.find_branch","page":"API reference","text":"find_branch(ps, from_bus_ext, to_bus_ext; ckt=nothing) -> branch index\n\nIndex in ps.branches of the branch (line or two-winding transformer) between PSS/E buses from_bus_ext and to_bus_ext, in either direction, with circuit id ckt (quotes/blanks ignored). Without ckt the branch must be unique: parallel circuits raise an ArgumentError listing their ids.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.find_branch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.add_trip_event!","page":"API reference","text":"add_trip_event!(sys, ev::TripLineEvent)\n\nRegister a line trip (build it with create_trip_line_event).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.add_trip_event!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.DisconnectDeviceEvent","page":"API reference","text":"DisconnectDeviceEvent\n\nDisconnect a device at a specified time by setting its online flag to false. The device's batch residual/Jacobian kernels skip it when online == false.\n\nFields:\n\ndevice_idx: index into psd.devices\nton: time at which the device is disconnected\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.DisconnectDeviceEvent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.add_disconnect_event!","page":"API reference","text":"add_disconnect_event!(sys, ev::DisconnectDeviceEvent)\n\nRegister a device disconnection. add_generator_trip! and add_load_trip! build these from PSS/E bus numbers and ids.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.add_disconnect_event!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.add_generator_trip!","page":"API reference","text":"add_generator_trip!(ps, bus_ext, id, t) -> Vector{DisconnectDeviceEvent}\n\nRemove the generator id at PSS/E bus bus_ext at t: its machine and all its controllers go offline together (see generator_devices); their states are frozen and the machine stops injecting current.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.add_generator_trip!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.generator_devices","page":"API reference","text":"generator_devices(ps, bus_ext, id) -> Vector{Int}\n\nIndices in ps.dynamic.devices that model the generator id at PSS/E bus bus_ext: its machine model and every controller (governor, exciter, stabilizer) attached to it (same bus and id, as used to wire them). A generator without a machine model is represented by the bus's StaticGenerator, returned only if it stands for this generator alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.generator_devices"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.add_load_trip!","page":"API reference","text":"add_load_trip!(ps, bus_ext, id, t) -> DisconnectDeviceEvent\n\nRemove (permanently disconnect) the load id at PSS/E bus bus_ext at t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.add_load_trip!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.LoadStepEvent","page":"API reference","text":"LoadStepEvent(device_idx, t, scale)\n\nAt time t, set the ZIPLoad psd.devices[device_idx] to scale times its INITIAL value (the p it had when the integration started): constant-power P, Q and the constant-impedance G, B all scale, so the total load at nominal voltage scales by scale. Steps do not compound: a later step on the same load with scale = 1.0 restores it. t must be at least one step (t >= dt); the parameters are restored when the integration returns. Build with create_load_step_event (external bus number + load id).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.LoadStepEvent"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.create_load_step_event","page":"API reference","text":"create_load_step_event(ps, bus_ext, id, t, scale) -> LoadStepEvent\n\nLoad step on the load id at PSS/E bus bus_ext: at time t it becomes scale times its initial value (e.g. 1.1 = +10 %, 0.0 = shed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.create_load_step_event"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.add_load_step_event!","page":"API reference","text":"add_load_step_event!(sys, ev::LoadStepEvent)\n\nRegister a load step (build it with create_load_step_event). The device must be a ZIP load and scale finite and >= 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.add_load_step_event!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.find_load_device","page":"API reference","text":"find_load_device(ps, bus_ext, id) -> device index\n\nIndex in ps.dynamic.devices of the ZIPLoad with PSS/E bus number bus_ext and load id id (compared after stripping quotes/blanks, as for machine ids). Errors if there is none or more than one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.find_load_device"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.KLUBackend","page":"API reference","text":"KLUBackend()\n\nCPU sparse LU from KLU.jl. Default for the CPU integrators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.KLUBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.CUDSSBackend","page":"API reference","text":"CUDSSBackend()\n\nNVIDIA cuDSS through CUDSS.jl (GPU only). Requires using CUDA, CUDSS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.CUDSSBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.SparseDirectSolverBackend","page":"API reference","text":"SparseDirectSolverBackend()\n\nSparseDirectSolver.jl, on whichever KernelAbstractions backend the matrix lives (CPU arrays or GPU arrays). Requires using SparseDirectSolver; GPU use also needs the GPU package (e.g. using CUDA).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.SparseDirectSolverBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.JacobianReuse","page":"API reference","text":"JacobianReuse(enabled; rho=0.1, max_stale=6)\n\nModified-Newton state shared by the Newton solves of one integration.\n\nWhen enabled, a Newton iteration reuses the factorization of the last Jacobian, across iterations and time steps, and refactors only if\n\nthere is no factorization for the current dt (first step; event re-solves at dt = 0 and the step after them),\nthe residual norm fell by less than 1/rho since the previous iteration,\nmax_stale iterations of the current solve have used one factorization.\n\nA solve that fails with reuse is retried from its initial guess with a fresh Jacobian every iteration (full Newton), so reuse cannot make a step fail that full Newton solves. dt is the step size of the current factorization (NaN when there is none).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.JacobianReuse"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.SolverLog","page":"API reference","text":"SolverLog\n\nLightweight event timer for the solver, modeled after PETSc PetscLogEvent. Tracks per-event call count and cumulative wall-clock time (in nanoseconds). Opt-in: pass to integrate! via the log kwarg; when nothing, zero overhead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.SolverLog"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#GradPower.NewtonConvergenceError","page":"API reference","text":"Thrown by the integrators when a Newton solve does not converge within its iteration limit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.NewtonConvergenceError"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#GradPower.DynamicNLP","page":"API reference","text":"GradPower.DynamicNLP(sys, dprob, tfinal, lvar, uvar; counters)\n\nConstruct the optional NLPModels model after loading NLPModels and MadNLP. The concrete model type lives in GradPowerNLPExt; this entry point is a constructor function, not a type. Forward objective evaluation is supported, but gradients still raise an error until discrete adjoints are implemented.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"GradPower.DynamicNLP"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/queries/#Queries-and-parameter-edits","page":"Queries and parameter edits","text":"Use external PSS/E bus numbers by default. Select numbering=:internal explicitly when passing an internal bus index; there is no automatic fallback. Device IDs accept surrounding quotes and blanks, so \"1\" and \" '1' \" select the same ordinary device. Model names are case-insensitive Symbols or strings (or device types); state and parameter names are case-sensitive.","title":"Queries and parameter edits"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/queries/#Find-devices-and-vector-indices","page":"Queries and parameter edits","text":"This example uses the bundled IEEE9 case:\n\nusing GradPower\nex = joinpath(pkgdir(GradPower), \"examples\")\nps = from_psse(joinpath(ex, \"ieee9_v33.raw\"),\n               joinpath(ex, \"ieee9bus_gov.dyr\"))\nbuild_network!(ps)\nrunpf!(ps)\ndp = DynamicProblem(ps)\ninitialize_dynamics!(dp, ps)\n\nb = bus_index(ps, 1)                         # internal bus index\nbus = get_bus(ps, b; numbering=:internal)     # Bus object\ngen = find_generator(ps, 1, \"1\")             # index into ps.gens\nload = find_load(ps, 5, \"1\")                 # index into ps.loads\nmachines = find_devices(ps; model=:genrou, online=true)\ndevice = find_device(ps, :genrou, 1, \"1\")     # dynamic device index\niw = state_index(ps, :genrou, 1, \"1\", :w)\nivr = state_index(ps, :bus, 1, \"\", :vr)\nip = parameter_index(ps, device, :H)\n@assert ip == parameter_index(ps, :genrou, 1, \"1\", :H)\n@assert get_index_info(iw, ps) isa StateChannel\n(speed=dp.zvec[iw], voltage_real=dp.zvec[ivr])\n(speed = 0.0, voltage_real = 1.04)\n\nfind_generator, find_load, and find_device require exactly one match; missing or ambiguous identities raise ArgumentError. find_devices returns all matching dynamic indices (possibly empty), with optional model, bus, id, and online filters. Static row indices and dynamic device indices are different index spaces.\n\nstate_index returns the actual global z index, not a device-local offset or a row in a selectively recorded trajectory. Network states use model :bus, ID \"\", and name :vr or :vi. parameter_index returns the global pvec index. Resolve indices again after rebuilding or changing the layout.","title":"Find devices and vector indices"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/queries/#Inspect-the-system","page":"Queries and parameter edits","text":"system_summary returns a vector of NamedTuple rows rather than printed text. Choose section=:buses (default), :generators, :loads, or :devices. Rows include an index, external bus number, identity, and section-specific data; device rows also include model, online status, and vector pointers.\n\nsystem_summary(ps; section=:devices)\n9-element Vector{NamedTuple}:\n (index = 1, model = :genrou, bus = 1, id = \"1\", online = true, diff_ptr = 1, alg_ptr = -26, ctrl_ptr = 1, par_ptr = 1)\n (index = 2, model = :genrou, bus = 2, id = \"1\", online = true, diff_ptr = 17, alg_ptr = -10, ctrl_ptr = 3, par_ptr = 15)\n (index = 3, model = :genrou, bus = 3, id = \"1\", online = true, diff_ptr = 33, alg_ptr = 6, ctrl_ptr = 5, par_ptr = 29)\n (index = 4, model = :ieesgo, bus = 1, id = \"1\", online = true, diff_ptr = 11, alg_ptr = -17, ctrl_ptr = 7, par_ptr = 43)\n (index = 5, model = :ieesgo, bus = 2, id = \"1\", online = true, diff_ptr = 27, alg_ptr = -1, ctrl_ptr = 8, par_ptr = 55)\n (index = 6, model = :ieesgo, bus = 3, id = \"1\", online = true, diff_ptr = 43, alg_ptr = 15, ctrl_ptr = 9, par_ptr = 67)\n (index = 7, model = :zipload, bus = 5, id = \"1\", online = true, diff_ptr = 34, alg_ptr = 16, ctrl_ptr = 10, par_ptr = 79)\n (index = 8, model = :zipload, bus = 6, id = \"1\", online = true, diff_ptr = 34, alg_ptr = 16, ctrl_ptr = 10, par_ptr = 88)\n (index = 9, model = :zipload, bus = 8, id = \"1\", online = true, diff_ptr = 34, alg_ptr = 16, ctrl_ptr = 10, par_ptr = 97)\n\nFor text output, use get_bus_info(1, ps) or display_parameters(ps). Both accept an IO destination first: get_bus_info(io, 1, ps) and display_parameters(io, ps). get_bus_info takes an external bus number. get_index_info(iw, ps) returns a StateChannel without printing; get_index_info(io, iw, ps) also prints it.","title":"Inspect the system"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/queries/#Edit-native-parameters-between-runs","page":"Queries and parameter edits","text":"get_parameter without dp reads the device configuration; with dp it reads that problem's live parameter vector. Both accept a dynamic device index or the (model, bus, id, name) selector:\n\nh = get_parameter(ps, device, :H)\n@assert h == get_parameter(dp, ps, :genrou, 1, \"1\", :H)\nset_parameter!(dp, ps, :genrou, 1, \"1\", :H, 1.05h)\n@assert get_parameter(dp, ps, device, :H) == 1.05h\ninitialize_dynamics!(dp, ps)\n@assert get_parameter(ps, device, :H) == 1.05h\nt, trajectory = integrate!(dp, ps, 0.1)\n\nset_parameter! accepts finite native parameter values and synchronizes the device configuration (dtype), the selected problem's pvec entry, and the matching CPU struct-of-arrays table field. Edits persist through initialize_dynamics!. Attached parameter values already use the model's internal, system-base conventions, not RAW/DYR machine-base values; do not apply a second base conversion.\n\nThe setter does not reset state. Reinitialize when a new equilibrium is desired. Initialization-derived pref/vref, all static-generator parameters, ZIPLoad pinj, qinj, v0mag, yreal, yimag, and IEEEST MODE/BUSR are read-only here. ESDC1A saturation parameters use the supported names sat_a and sat_b. Inspect names with get_param_names or display_parameters.\n\nwarning: CPU, between runs only\nNever call these setters during integration, including from callbacks. set_parameter! is for a CPU DynamicProblem, not GPU or batched problems. Other DynamicProblem instances and GPU/batch layouts sharing ps are not synchronized: recreate them after an edit.","title":"Edit native parameters between runs"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/queries/#Change-the-operating-point-before-power-flow","page":"Queries and parameter edits","text":"Use set_load! for static pd/qd; it also synchronizes mapped ZIPLoad configurations and tables. Use set_generator! for static psch/qsch schedules and qmin/qmax reactive limits. Omitted keywords leave values unchanged. Powers and limits are in pu on the system base; reactive limits may be infinite.\n\nContinuing the example, discard the old problem and solve the new operating point before allocating dynamics:\n\npd = ps.loads[load].pd\npsch = ps.gens[gen].psch\nset_load!(ps, 5, \"1\"; pd=1.01pd)\nset_generator!(ps, 1, \"1\"; psch=psch, qmin=-10.0, qmax=10.0)\nrunpf!(ps)\ndp = DynamicProblem(ps)                     # new problem, not the old dp\ninitialize_dynamics!(dp, ps)\n\nThese are pre-power-flow edits only, not events or mid-run controls. After either setter, run runpf!, construct a new DynamicProblem, and initialize it; recreate any other problems or GPU/batch layouts sharing ps. Neither setter edits topology, bus type, status, or machine base (mbase). Use the event API for supported scheduled disturbances.","title":"Change the operating point before power flow"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/batched/#Many-scenarios-at-once","page":"Many scenarios at once","text":"Studies rarely need one simulation. Typical batches are:\n\nmany loading levels,\nmany parameter values,\nmany contingencies.\n\nGradPower.integrate_batched! integrates M scenarios that share the same network and events but have their own initial state bl.z[m, :] and parameters bl.p[m, :].\n\nThreads: scenarios run in parallel on Julia threads (start Julia with -t auto).\nSame result as one-by-one: each scenario's trajectory is bit-for-bit what integrate! gives for it alone.","title":"Many scenarios at once"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/batched/#A-load-sweep","page":"Many scenarios at once","text":"using GradPower, CairoMakie, Printf\nex = joinpath(pkgdir(GradPower), \"examples\")\nsys = from_psse(joinpath(ex, \"ieee9_v33.raw\"), joinpath(ex, \"ieee9bus_gov.dyr\"))\nbuild_network!(sys); runpf!(sys)\ndp = DynamicProblem(sys); initialize_dynamics!(dp, sys)\nadd_event!(sys, ContingencyEvent(sys.busmap[7], 0.02, 0.1, 0.2))\n\nM  = 6\nbl = GradPower.BatchedLayout(dp, sys, M)       # M copies of (z, u, p)\n\n# scenario m: every load scaled by s_m (P, Q and the impedance part)\nzl = sys.dynamic.layout.zipload\nscales = range(0.8, 1.2, length = M)\nfor m in 1:M, k in 1:zl.n, o in (0, 1, 7, 8)   # pinj, qinj, yreal, yimag\n    bl.p[m, Int(zl.par_ptr[k]) + o] *= scales[m]\nend\n\nt, trajs = GradPower.integrate_batched!(bl, sys, 3.0)\nlength(trajs), size(trajs[1])\n(6, (66, 361))\n\nw2 = GradPower.gen_speeds(sys)[2]\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 340))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"time (s)\", ylabel = \"gen 2 speed deviation (pu)\")\nfor m in 1:M\n    lines!(ax, t, trajs[m][w2, :], label = @sprintf(\"load × %.2f\", scales[m]))\nend\naxislegend(ax)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nOnly the parameters were scaled, so each scenario starts from the operating point of the base case and drifts toward a new equilibrium. To start each scenario at its own equilibrium, solve the power flow and initialize once per loading level, then copy the result into bl.z[m, :] and bl.p[m, :]. Three things must be scaled together:\n\nthe static loads, used by the power flow;\nthe ZIP load devices, used by the dynamics;\nthe generation, so the slack does not pick up the whole change.\n\nfunction operating_point(scale)\n    s = from_psse(joinpath(ex, \"ieee9_v33.raw\"), joinpath(ex, \"ieee9bus_gov.dyr\"))\n    slack = Set(i for (i, b) in enumerate(s.buses) if b.type == 3)\n    for l in s.loads; l.pd *= scale; l.qd *= scale; end\n    for g in s.gens; g.bus in slack || (g.psch *= scale); end\n    for d in s.dynamic.devices\n        d.dtype isa GradPower.ZIPLoad || continue\n        d.dtype.pinj *= scale; d.dtype.qinj *= scale\n    end\n    build_network!(s); runpf!(s)\n    d = DynamicProblem(s); initialize_dynamics!(d, s)\n    return d.zvec, d.pvec\nend\nfor m in 1:M\n    z, p = operating_point(scales[m])\n    bl.z[m, :] .= z; bl.p[m, :] .= p\nend\nt, trajs = GradPower.integrate_batched!(bl, sys, 3.0)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 340))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"time (s)\", ylabel = \"gen 2 speed deviation (pu)\")\nfor m in 1:M\n    lines!(ax, t, trajs[m][w2, :], label = @sprintf(\"load × %.2f\", scales[m]))\nend\naxislegend(ax)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nNow every scenario is flat until the fault, and the heavier cases swing harder. scripts/dynstab_io.jl (apply_load_scale!) does the same for the dataset pipeline, including resetting the generators' reactive-power limits between levels.\n\nnote: Same device layout\nAll scenarios of one batch must have the same device layout: the same machines and controllers, in the same order. Only values (states and parameters) may differ.","title":"A load sweep"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/batched/#Per-scenario-output","page":"Many scenarios at once","text":"The recording options work for batches too:\n\nsave_every, save_idxs and save_eltype apply to every scenario;\nstep_callback(m, k, t, z) is called for each scenario m. With threads it runs concurrently, so it must only touch data belonging to scenario m.\n\npeak = zeros(M)                                   # max |speed dev.| per scenario\ncb = (m, k, t, z) -> (peak[m] = max(peak[m], abs(z[w2])); nothing)\nGradPower.integrate_batched!(bl, sys, 3.0; save_idxs = Int[], step_callback = cb)\nround.(peak, digits = 5)\n6-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.02478\n 0.01878\n 0.01471\n 0.01314\n 0.01221\n 0.01651","title":"Per-scenario output"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/batched/#Batches-on-the-GPU","page":"Many scenarios at once","text":"On an NVIDIA GPU the same kind of batch can run entirely on the device, where it pays off for large networks. See Linear solvers and GPU.","title":"Batches on the GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/integrators/#Integrators-and-Newton-options","page":"Integrators and Newton options","text":"integrate! advances the DAE\n\ndot x = f(x y v) qquad 0 = g(x y v)\n\nwith a fixed step dt (default 1/120 s, i.e. two steps per 60 Hz cycle). Every step is an implicit nonlinear system solved by Newton's method. You choose:\n\nthe integration formula (integrator);\nhow the Newton system is solved (solver, linear_solver);\nhow much Newton work is reused (jacobian_reuse, predictor).\n\nNone of these options change what is simulated, only accuracy and cost.","title":"Integrators and Newton options"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/integrators/#Backward-Euler-and-Crank–Nicolson","page":"Integrators and Newton options","text":"integrator formula on differential states order\n:backward_euler (default) x_k+1 = x_k + h f(z_k+1) 1\n:crank_nicolson x_k+1 = x_k + tfrach2big(f(z_k+1) + f(z_k)big) 2\n\nIn both cases the algebraic equations hold exactly at every step.\n\nBackward Euler strongly damps fast modes. That makes it robust, but it also damps the electromechanical oscillations you may want to see.\nCrank–Nicolson preserves them much better. For the same accuracy it can take steps several times larger.\n\nThe error after 0.5 s of a smooth transient (a governor setpoint step), against a very fine reference:\n\nusing GradPower, CairoMakie\nex = joinpath(pkgdir(GradPower), \"examples\")\nsys = from_psse(joinpath(ex, \"ieee9_v33.raw\"), joinpath(ex, \"ieee9bus_gov.dyr\"))\nbuild_network!(sys); runpf!(sys)\ndp = DynamicProblem(sys); initialize_dynamics!(dp, sys)\nz0 = copy(dp.zvec); p0 = copy(dp.pvec)\n\nig = sys.dynamic.layout.ieesgo                  # +0.05 pu on every governor reference\nfor k in 1:ig.n\n    dp.pvec[Int(ig.par_ptr[k]) + 11] += 0.05\nend\n\nfinal(m, h) = (dp.zvec .= z0; integrate!(dp, sys, 0.5; dt = h, integrator = m)[2][:, end])\nref = final(:crank_nicolson, 1/1920)\nhs  = [1/30, 1/60, 1/120, 1/240]\nerr(m) = [maximum(abs.(final(m, h) .- ref)) for h in hs]\n\nfig = Figure(size = (620, 420))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xscale = log10, yscale = log10, xlabel = \"dt (s)\",\n          ylabel = \"max error at t = 0.5 s\", xticks = (hs, [\"1/30\", \"1/60\", \"1/120\", \"1/240\"]))\nscatterlines!(ax, hs, err(:backward_euler), label = \"backward Euler\")\nscatterlines!(ax, hs, err(:crank_nicolson), label = \"Crank–Nicolson\")\nlines!(ax, hs, 0.1 .* hs, color = :gray, linestyle = :dash, label = \"slope 1\")\nlines!(ax, hs, 0.3 .* hs .^ 2, color = :gray, linestyle = :dot, label = \"slope 2\")\naxislegend(ax, position = :rb)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nHalving dt halves the backward-Euler error and divides the Crank–Nicolson error by four.\n\nThe difference shows up clearly through a fault on a coarse grid (dt = 1/30 s):\n\ndp.pvec .= p0                                     # undo the setpoint step\nadd_event!(sys, ContingencyEvent(sys.busmap[7], 0.02, 0.1, 0.2))\nw2 = GradPower.gen_speeds(sys)[2]\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 330))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"time (s)\", ylabel = \"gen 2 speed deviation (pu)\")\ndp.zvec .= z0; t, r = integrate!(dp, sys, 3.0; dt = 1/960, integrator = :crank_nicolson)\nlines!(ax, t, r[w2, :], color = :black, linewidth = 3, label = \"reference (dt = 1/960)\")\nfor (m, c) in ((:backward_euler, :tomato), (:crank_nicolson, :dodgerblue))\n    dp.zvec .= z0; t, r = integrate!(dp, sys, 3.0; dt = 1/30, integrator = m)\n    scatterlines!(ax, t, r[w2, :], color = c, markersize = 5, label = \"$m, dt = 1/30\")\nend\naxislegend(ax)\nempty!(sys.dynamic.events)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nBackward Euler visibly damps the swing. Crank–Nicolson stays close to the reference even with only 4 steps per oscillation.\n\nnote: Note\nCrank–Nicolson cannot be combined with limit_method (TGOV1 valve limits), because the limit equations are formulated for backward Euler.","title":"Backward Euler and Crank–Nicolson"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/integrators/#Newton:-reuse-and-prediction","page":"Integrators and Newton options","text":"Each step solves F(z) = 0 with Newton iterations J Δz = -F. Two options, both on by default, cut the cost:\n\njacobian_reuse = true keeps the factorized Jacobian across iterations and time steps (modified Newton). It refactors only when convergence slows, after an event, or after a failure. A step that fails with reuse is retried with full Newton, so reuse never makes a step fail.\npredictor = true starts each step from the linear extrapolation 2 z_k − z_{k−1} (not across events).\n\nBoth converge to the same newton_tol (default 1e-10), so the trajectories agree to that tolerance. SolverLog counts the work:\n\nadd_event!(sys, ContingencyEvent(sys.busmap[7], 0.02, 0.1, 0.2))\nwork = map([(true, true), (false, false)]) do (reuse, pred)\n    log = SolverLog()\n    dp.zvec .= z0\n    integrate!(dp, sys, 3.0; jacobian_reuse = reuse, predictor = pred, log = log)\n    (reuse = reuse, predictor = pred, residuals = log.residual_count,\n     factorizations = log.lsolve_factor_count)\nend\nempty!(sys.dynamic.events)\nwork\n2-element Vector{@NamedTuple{reuse::Bool, predictor::Bool, residuals::Int64, factorizations::Int64}}:\n (reuse = 1, predictor = 1, residuals = 1770, factorizations = 83)\n (reuse = 0, predictor = 0, residuals = 1125, factorizations = 763)\n\nOn large systems, factorizations dominate the cost, so reuse is the main speed-up.","title":"Newton: reuse and prediction"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/integrators/#Solver-structure","page":"Integrators and Newton options","text":"solver chooses how each Newton system is solved:\n\nsolver method when\n:monolithic (default) sparse LU of the full Jacobian general; supports limits\n:schur eliminate each device cluster (dense, small), sparse LU of the reduced network system large systems, GPU-style structure\n:schur_gmres Schur reduction + GMRES preconditioned by the network admittance experimental\n\nAll three give the same trajectories (to Newton tolerance). The sparse factorization itself is chosen with linear_solver (see Linear solvers and GPU).","title":"Solver structure"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/integrators/#When-Newton-fails","page":"Integrators and Newton options","text":"If a step does not converge, integrate! throws a GradPower.NewtonConvergenceError with the time and the kind of solve. The system is restored even in that case. Typical causes are a zero-impedance fault, a collapsing voltage, or a dt far too large for the case.","title":"When Newton fails"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/events/#Contingencies-and-events","page":"Contingencies and events","text":"Events are registered on the system before calling integrate!. They are applied between time steps: the step that lands on the event time is computed with the old equations, then the event changes the system and the algebraic equations are re-solved. Everything is undone when integrate! returns, so the same sys can be reused.\n\nDevices are addressed the way they appear in the PSS/E files: external bus number + id. Quotes and blanks in ids are ignored (\"1\", \"'1 '\" and 1 are the same).\n\nevent constructor effect\nbus fault ContingencyEvent(bus, r, ton, toff; xfault) shunt 1/(r + jx) to ground during [ton, toff)\nline trip create_trip_line_event(sys, from, to, t; ckt, toff) + add_trip_event! branch removed at t (reclosed at toff)\ngenerator trip add_generator_trip!(sys, bus, id, t) machine + its governor/exciter/stabilizer offline\nload trip add_load_trip!(sys, bus, id, t) load disconnected\nload step create_load_step_event(sys, bus, id, t, scale) + add_load_step_event! load becomes scale × its initial value\n\nWe use the New England 39-bus system (10 machines with governors) and plot the machine speeds for each kind of event.\n\nusing GradPower, CairoMakie\nex = joinpath(pkgdir(GradPower), \"examples\")\nsys = from_psse(joinpath(ex, \"IEEE39.raw\"), joinpath(ex, \"IEEE39_gov.dyr\"))\nbuild_network!(sys); runpf!(sys)\ndp = DynamicProblem(sys); initialize_dynamics!(dp, sys)\nz0 = copy(dp.zvec)\nw  = GradPower.gen_speeds(sys)\n\nfunction clear_events!(sys)\n    d = sys.dynamic\n    foreach(empty!, (d.events, d.disconnect_events, d.trip_events, d.load_step_events))\nend\n\nfunction speeds_plot(pos, title; tf = 10.0)\n    dp.zvec .= z0\n    t, tr = integrate!(dp, sys, tf)\n    ax = Axis(pos, title = title, xlabel = \"time (s)\", ylabel = \"speed dev. (pu)\")\n    foreach(i -> lines!(ax, t, tr[i, :]), w)\n    clear_events!(sys)\n    return t, tr\nend","title":"Contingencies and events"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/events/#Faults","page":"Contingencies and events","text":"A resistive fault takes r only; xfault adds a reactance. A fault with zero impedance cannot be represented (infinite admittance) and is rejected: use a small impedance instead.\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 330))\nadd_event!(sys, ContingencyEvent(sys.busmap[16], 0.05, 1.0, 1.1))\nspeeds_plot(fig[1, 1], \"resistive fault, bus 16, r = 0.05\")\nadd_event!(sys, ContingencyEvent(sys.busmap[16], 0.01, 1.0, 1.1; xfault = 0.05))\nspeeds_plot(fig[1, 2], \"impedance fault, bus 16, z = 0.01 + j0.05\")\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Faults"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/events/#Line-trips-and-parallel-circuits","page":"Contingencies and events","text":"Lines are found by their two PSS/E buses (in either order) and, when there are several in parallel, by their circuit id ckt. Without ckt on a parallel pair, GradPower refuses to guess and lists the circuits. A line can be reclosed by passing toff.\n\nfind_branch(sys, 16, 17)          # index into sys.branches\n21\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 330))\nadd_trip_event!(sys, create_trip_line_event(sys, 16, 17, 1.0))\nspeeds_plot(fig[1, 1], \"line 16–17 tripped at 1 s\")\nadd_trip_event!(sys, create_trip_line_event(sys, 16, 17, 1.0; toff = 1.5))\nspeeds_plot(fig[1, 2], \"line 16–17 tripped at 1 s, reclosed at 1.5 s\")\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Line trips and parallel circuits"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/events/#Generator-and-load-outages","page":"Contingencies and events","text":"A generator trip disconnects the machine and every controller attached to it. Their states freeze at their last value, and the machine stops injecting current. generator_devices shows what will go offline:\n\n[nameof(typeof(sys.dynamic.devices[i].dtype)) for i in generator_devices(sys, 38, \"1\")]\n2-element Vector{Symbol}:\n :Genrou\n :IEESGO\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 330))\nadd_generator_trip!(sys, 38, \"1\", 1.0)\nspeeds_plot(fig[1, 1], \"generator at bus 38 tripped (830 MW)\")\nadd_load_trip!(sys, 20, \"1\", 1.0)\nspeeds_plot(fig[1, 2], \"load at bus 20 tripped (680 MW)\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nLosing generation makes the frequency fall, and losing load makes it rise. The governors then act on it. The tripped machine's speed is the flat line in the left plot.","title":"Generator and load outages"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/events/#Load-steps","page":"Contingencies and events","text":"Load steps are relative to the initial load (both the constant-power and the constant-impedance parts scale). They do not compound: a later step with scale = 1.0 restores the load.\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 330))\nadd_load_step_event!(sys, create_load_step_event(sys, 20, \"1\", 1.0, 1.3))\nspeeds_plot(fig[1, 1], \"load 20: +30 % at 1 s\")\nadd_load_step_event!(sys, create_load_step_event(sys, 20, \"1\", 1.0, 1.3))\nadd_load_step_event!(sys, create_load_step_event(sys, 20, \"1\", 4.0, 1.0))\nspeeds_plot(fig[1, 2], \"load 20: +30 % at 1 s, back to 100 % at 4 s\")\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Load steps"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/events/#Combining-events","page":"Contingencies and events","text":"Any number of events of any kind can be registered together. A classic sequence is a fault, followed by its clearing through a line trip:\n\nfig = Figure(size = (900, 330))\nadd_event!(sys, ContingencyEvent(sys.busmap[16], 0.01, 1.0, 1.1))\nadd_trip_event!(sys, create_trip_line_event(sys, 16, 17, 1.1))\nt, tr = speeds_plot(fig[1, 1], \"fault at bus 16, cleared by tripping line 16–17\")\nnet = sys.dynamic.diff_dim + sys.dynamic.alg_dim\nax = Axis(fig[1, 2], title = \"voltage at buses 16 and 17\", xlabel = \"time (s)\", ylabel = \"|V| (pu)\")\nfor b in (16, 17)\n    i = sys.busmap[b]\n    lines!(ax, t, hypot.(tr[net + 2i - 1, :], tr[net + 2i, :]), label = \"bus $b\")\nend\naxislegend(ax, position = :rb)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nnote: Where events run\nAll events work with the CPU integrators (integrate! with every solver, and integrate_batched!). The GPU integrators currently apply resistive bus faults only, and raise an error if any other event is registered.","title":"Combining events"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#How-it-works","page":"How it works","text":"This page summarizes the model and the numerical method. Each section points to where it is implemented.","title":"How it works"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#The-DAE","page":"How it works","text":"A power system with machines, controllers and loads is a semi-explicit differential-algebraic system:\n\nbeginaligned\ndot x = f(x y v)             textmachine and controller dynamics \n0      = g(x y v)             textdevice algebraic equations (stator governor outputs ) \n0      = Y v - I(x y v)       textnetwork current balance at every bus\nendaligned\n\nx: differential states, for example the rotor angle delta, speed omega, and internal EMFs e_q e_d of each machine.\ny: algebraic device states.\nv: bus voltages in rectangular form v_b = v_rb + j v_ib.\nY: the network admittance matrix.\n\nThe network equation is written in current-injection form: each device injects a current I_b(x y v) at its bus, and Kirchhoff's law gives Y v = I. A ZIP load with impedance part y_L injects -y_L v_b - overline(S_L  v_b). A fault with impedance r + jx injects -v_b  (r + jx).","title":"The DAE"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Initialization","page":"How it works","text":"runpf! solves the steady state Y v = overlineS  v at given generation and load. It enforces generator reactive limits by switching PV buses to PQ. initialize_dynamics! then inverts each device's equations at that operating point. It finds machine internal states that inject exactly the power-flow current, and controller setpoints (governor reference, exciter voltage reference) that hold them there. The result satisfies f = 0 and g = 0 to machine precision, so an undisturbed simulation stays flat.","title":"Initialization"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Time-stepping","page":"How it works","text":"With step h, backward Euler (BE) and Crank–Nicolson (CN) both lead, at every step, to one nonlinear system in z_k+1 = (x y v)_k+1:\n\nF(z) = beginbmatrix x - a - c f(z)  g(z)  Y v - I(z) endbmatrix = 0\nqquad\n(a c) = begincases (x_k h)  textBE  (x_k + tfrac h2 f(z_k) tfrac h2)  textCN endcases\n\nBoth methods share the same Newton machinery and only differ in the anchor a and the coefficient c. The Jacobian is\n\nJ = beginbmatrix I - cf_x  -cf_y  -cf_v  g_x  g_y  g_v  -I_x  -I_y  Y - I_v endbmatrix\n\nEvents (a fault switching on, a line tripping, ...) change F. They are applied between steps, followed by a re-solve of the algebraic equations (c = 0), so the next step starts from a consistent state. This handles the jump in voltages at a fault.","title":"Time stepping"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Newton's-method-and-its-cost","page":"How it works","text":"Each step runs Newton iterations J Delta z = -F(z). For large networks the sparse LU factorization of J dominates. Two cheap savings:\n\nModified Newton: reuse the factorization across iterations and steps while convergence is fast (F drops by more than ×10 per iteration).\nPredictor: start from the extrapolation 2z_k - z_k-1.\n\nOn ACTIVSg2000 together they cut factorizations from about 1200 to about 10 per 5 s simulation.","title":"Newton's method and its cost"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Structure:-clusters-and-Schur-reduction","page":"How it works","text":"Order the unknowns by cluster: each machine together with its governor, exciter and stabilizer, and finally the bus voltages. A cluster only touches the voltage of its own bus, so J has an arrow shape:\n\nJ = beginbmatrix A_1    B_1   ddots   vdots    A_N  B_N  C_1  cdots  C_N  D endbmatrix\nqquad\nS = D - sum_k C_k A_k^-1 B_k \n\nThe blocks: each A_k is small and dense. B_k and C_k couple the cluster to its bus.\nElimination: eliminating the clusters leaves the Schur complement S. It has exactly the sparsity of the admittance matrix Y, because each correction lands on one bus's diagonal block.\nOne Newton step then costs:\nN independent small dense LUs, which batch perfectly on a GPU;\none sparse LU of a network-sized matrix;\nback-substitution.\n\nThis is solver = :schur on the CPU and integrate_gpu_schur_cudss! on the GPU. :schur_gmres instead solves the reduced system iteratively, preconditioned by the network admittance alone, which never needs refactoring.","title":"Structure: clusters and Schur reduction"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Kernels-and-layout","page":"How it works","text":"Device equations are not written per device instance. Each device type stores its instances as a structure of arrays (a column per parameter). One kernel evaluates the residual, and one the Jacobian entries, for all instances of that type. The same kernels run on the CPU and, through KernelAbstractions.jl, on the GPU. The Jacobian's sparsity pattern is built once, and each kernel writes into precomputed positions of its nonzeros, so assembly allocates nothing.","title":"Kernels and layout"},{"category":"section","location":"theory/#Where-to-look-in-the-source","page":"How it works","text":"topic file\npower flow src/pflow.jl\nresidual, Jacobian, time loop, events src/dynamics.jl\nNewton, Jacobian reuse src/numerics.jl\nclusters, Schur reduction src/clusters.jl, src/schur.jl\ndevice kernels / tables src/kernels/, src/tables/\nbatched CPU src/batched.jl\nGPU ext/GradPowerCUDAExt/","title":"Where to look in the source"},{"category":"section","location":"#GradPower.jl","page":"Home","text":"Power-system dynamic simulation in Julia, on CPU and GPU, from standard PSS/E files.\n\nGradPower reads a power network (.raw) and its dynamic models (.dyr), solves the power flow, and integrates the differential-algebraic equations (DAE) of the whole system through faults, line trips, generator and load outages, and load changes. It is built to run many simulations: scenarios can be batched on CPU threads or on an NVIDIA GPU, and the output can be reduced to exactly what a study or a machine-learning dataset needs.\n\nusing GradPower, CairoMakie\nex = joinpath(pkgdir(GradPower), \"examples\")\n\nsys = from_psse(joinpath(ex, \"IEEE39.raw\"), joinpath(ex, \"IEEE39_gov.dyr\"))\nbuild_network!(sys); runpf!(sys)\ndp = DynamicProblem(sys); initialize_dynamics!(dp, sys)\n\n# 100 ms three-phase fault at bus 16, then simulate 8 s\nadd_event!(sys, ContingencyEvent(sys.busmap[16], 0.01, 1.0, 1.1))\nt, traj = integrate!(dp, sys, 8.0)\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 300))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"time (s)\", ylabel = \"rotor speed deviation (pu)\",\n          title = \"New England 39-bus system, 10 machines\")\nfor i in GradPower.gen_speeds(sys)\n    lines!(ax, t, traj[i, :])\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"GradPower.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-you-can-do","page":"Home","text":" \nSimulate Fault-on / fault-off transients with GENROU machines, governors, exciters and stabilizers (First simulation).\nDisturb Resistive or impedance faults, line trips with circuit ids, generator trips (with their controllers), load trips and load steps (Contingencies and events).\nScale out Run many scenarios in one call on CPU threads or the GPU (Many scenarios at once, Linear solvers and GPU).\nChoose accuracy Backward Euler or second-order Crank–Nicolson, with Jacobian reuse and a predictor (Integrators).\nStore less Record only some states, every k-th step, in Float32; stop unstable runs early (Recording and early stop).\nBuild datasets Sweep loads × faults and write labelled HDF5 files for training (Generating training data).","title":"What you can do"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"GradPower is not registered yet. From the Julia REPL:\n\npkg> add https://github.com/dynsytms/GradPower.jl\n\nFor the GPU path also add CUDA and CUDSS (see Linear solvers and GPU).","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-start","page":"Home","text":"First simulation: the five steps of every study.\nReading the results: where every quantity lives in the output.\nContingencies and events: everything you can schedule.\nHow it works: the equations and the numerical method, for the curious.\n\nThe bundled test systems live in the examples/ folder of the package: 2bus, ieee9_v33 (WSCC 9-bus) and IEEE39 (New England), with several .dyr variants (governors, exciters, stabilizers).","title":"Where to start"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_simulation/#First-simulation","page":"First simulation","text":"Every GradPower study follows the same five steps:\n\n .raw + .dyr ──► from_psse ──► build_network! ──► runpf! ──► DynamicProblem\n                                                              initialize_dynamics!\n                                                                     │\n                       add_event! (faults, trips, ...) ──────────────┤\n                                                                     ▼\n                                                     integrate!  ──►  (t, traj)\n\nWe go through them on the WSCC 9-bus system: three GENROU machines, each with an IEESGO steam governor, and three loads.","title":"First simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_simulation/#1.-Read-the-case","page":"First simulation","text":"using GradPower, CairoMakie\nex = joinpath(pkgdir(GradPower), \"examples\")\n\nsys = from_psse(joinpath(ex, \"ieee9_v33.raw\"), joinpath(ex, \"ieee9bus_gov.dyr\"))\nPowerSystem:\n  baseMVA: 100.0\n\nBuses:\n================================================================\ni\tid\t\t\ttype\tbaseKV\tv0m\tv0a\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n1\t'BUS1        '\t\t3\t16.5\t1.04\t0.000e+00\n2\t'BUS2        '\t\t2\t18.0\t1.025\t1.620e-01\n3\t'BUS3        '\t\t2\t13.8\t1.025\t8.142e-02\n4\t'BUS4        '\t\t1\t230.0\t1.02579\t-3.869e-02\n5\t'BUS5        '\t\t1\t230.0\t0.99563\t-6.962e-02\n6\t'BUS6        '\t\t1\t230.0\t1.01265\t-6.436e-02\n7\t'BUS7        '\t\t1\t230.0\t1.02577\t6.492e-02\n8\t'BUS8        '\t\t1\t230.0\t1.01588\t1.270e-02\n9\t'BUS9        '\t\t1\t230.0\t1.03235\t3.433e-02\n\nGens:\n===============================================================\nbus\tid\t\tpsch\t\tqsch\t\tmbase\n---------------------------------------------------------------\n1\t1\t\t7.164e-01\t2.705e-01\t260.0\n2\t1\t\t1.630e+00\t6.654e-02\t310.0\n3\t1\t\t8.500e-01\t-1.086e-01\t280.0\n\nLoads:\n===================================\nbus\tid\t\tpd\tqd\n-----------------------------------\n5\t1\t\t1.25\t-0.5\n6\t1\t\t0.9\t-0.3\n8\t1\t\t1.0\t-0.35\n\nBranches:\n=============================================================================================\nfr\tto\tid\t\tr\tx\tsh\ttap\tshift\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n4\t5\t'1 '\t\t0.01\t0.085\t0.176\t0.0\t0.0\n4\t6\t'1 '\t\t0.017\t0.092\t0.158\t0.0\t0.0\n5\t7\t'1 '\t\t0.032\t0.161\t0.306\t0.0\t0.0\n6\t9\t'1 '\t\t0.039\t0.17\t0.358\t0.0\t0.0\n7\t8\t'1 '\t\t0.0085\t0.072\t0.149\t0.0\t0.0\n8\t9\t'1 '\t\t0.0119\t0.1008\t0.209\t0.0\t0.0\n1\t4\t'T1'\t\t0.0\t0.0576\t0.0\t1.0\t0.0\n2\t7\t'T2'\t\t0.0\t0.0625\t0.0\t1.0\t0.0\n9\t3\t'T3'\t\t0.0\t0.0586\t0.0\t1.0\t0.0\n\nShunts:\n==============================\nbus\tid\t\tgsh\tbsh\n------------------------------\n\n\nfrom_psse parses the network (buses, lines, transformers, generators, loads) and attaches a dynamic model to each device. Generators found in the .dyr get their machine and controller models. Loads become ZIP loads, constant impedance by default. Generators without a dynamic record become StaticGenerator stand-ins that hold their bus voltage.","title":"1. Read the case"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_simulation/#2.-Build-the-network-and-solve-the-power-flow","page":"First simulation","text":"build_network!(sys)          # admittance matrix Y\nrunpf!(sys)                  # Newton power flow (with generator Q limits)\n[(b.i, round(b.v0m, digits = 4), round(rad2deg(b.v0a), digits = 2)) for b in sys.buses]\n9-element Vector{Tuple{Int64, Float64, Float64}}:\n (1, 1.04, 0.0)\n (2, 1.025, 9.28)\n (3, 1.025, 4.66)\n (4, 1.0258, -2.22)\n (5, 0.9956, -3.99)\n (6, 1.0127, -3.69)\n (7, 1.0258, 3.72)\n (8, 1.0159, 0.73)\n (9, 1.0324, 1.97)\n\nThe table lists the PSS/E bus number, the voltage magnitude (pu) and the angle (degrees) of the operating point.","title":"2. Build the network and solve the power flow"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_simulation/#3.-Initialize-the-dynamics","page":"First simulation","text":"dp = DynamicProblem(sys)\ninitialize_dynamics!(dp, sys)\nlength(dp.zvec)              # number of unknowns of the DAE\n66\n\ninitialize_dynamics! computes every machine's internal state, every controller's setpoint and every load's admittance so that the system sits exactly at the power-flow equilibrium. A simulation without any event stays flat:\n\nt, traj = integrate!(dp, sys, 2.0)\nmaximum(abs.(traj .- traj[:, 1]))     # drift over 2 s\n3.261058978409892e-9","title":"3. Initialize the dynamics"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_simulation/#4.-Schedule-a-fault","page":"First simulation","text":"ContingencyEvent is a shunt fault at an (internal) bus index. sys.busmap converts PSS/E bus numbers to internal indices.\n\nadd_event!(sys, ContingencyEvent(sys.busmap[7], 0.02, 0.1, 0.2))  # r = 0.02 pu, 0.1 s → 0.2 s\n1-element Vector{ContingencyEvent}:\n ContingencyEvent(7, false, 0.02, 0.1, 0.2, 0.0)","title":"4. Schedule a fault"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_simulation/#5.-Integrate","page":"First simulation","text":"dp.zvec .= traj[:, 1]                 # restart from the equilibrium\nt, traj = integrate!(dp, sys, 5.0)    # dt = 1/120 s by default\nsize(traj)\n(66, 601)\n\ntraj[:, j] is the full state at time t[j]. Plot the machine speeds and every bus voltage:\n\nnet = sys.dynamic.diff_dim + sys.dynamic.alg_dim       # voltages come last\nvm(b) = hypot.(traj[net + 2b - 1, :], traj[net + 2b, :])\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 520))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], ylabel = \"speed deviation (pu)\")\nfor (k, i) in enumerate(GradPower.gen_speeds(sys))\n    lines!(ax1, t, traj[i, :], label = \"gen $k\")\nend\naxislegend(ax1)\nax2 = Axis(fig[2, 1], xlabel = \"time (s)\", ylabel = \"|V| (pu)\")\nfor b in eachindex(sys.buses)\n    lines!(ax2, t, vm(b), label = \"bus $(sys.buses[b].i)\")\nend\nfig[2, 2] = Legend(fig, ax2, framevisible = false)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nDuring the fault, voltages collapse near bus 7. The machines accelerate, swing against each other after the fault is cleared, and the governors bring the frequency back.\n\ntip: Running it again\nintegrate! leaves sys as it found it: faults are switched off, tripped lines restored, parameters reset. To repeat a study, reset dp.zvec to the initial state (or call initialize_dynamics! again), and clear or replace the events in sys.dynamic.events.","title":"5. Integrate"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/first_simulation/#Variations-to-try","page":"First simulation","text":"integrate!(dp, sys, 5.0; dt = 1/240)                     # smaller step\nintegrate!(dp, sys, 5.0; integrator = :crank_nicolson)   # 2nd-order method\nintegrate!(dp, sys, 5.0; solver = :schur)                # Schur-reduced Newton\nintegrate!(dp, sys, 5.0; save_every = 4)                 # store every 4th step\n\nEach option is explained in the guides.","title":"Variations to try"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#Supported-models-and-input-files","page":"Supported models and input files","text":"","title":"Supported models and input files"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#Input-files","page":"Supported models and input files","text":"format read with contains\nPSS/E .raw (v33) from_psse(raw, dyr) buses, loads, fixed shunts, generators, lines, 2- and 3-winding transformers\nPSS/E .dyr from_psse(raw, dyr) machine and controller models\nMATPOWER .m GradPower.read_matpower_case(file) static network (power flow only)\n\nfrom_psse(raw, nothing) reads a network without dynamics, for power flow alone.","title":"Input files"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#Device-models","page":"Supported models and input files","text":"PSS/E model kind differential / algebraic states\nGENROU round-rotor synchronous machine (with saturation) 6 / 4\nGENSAL salient-pole machine (mapped onto GENROU with x_q' = x_d', T_q0' = T_d0') 6 / 4\nIEESGO steam turbine-governor 5 / 1\nTGOV1 steam turbine-governor (with optional valve limits) 2 / 1\nSEXS simplified excitation system 2 / 0\nESDC1A DC exciter 3 / 0\nIEEEST power system stabilizer 7 / 1\nloads ZIP load: constant impedance (default), current, power 0 / 0\ngenerators without .dyr record StaticGenerator: holds P and the bus voltage 0 / 0–2\n\nControllers are attached to their machine by bus and id, as in PSS/E.","title":"Device models"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#Checking-what-a-.dyr-file-uses","page":"Supported models and input files","text":"Real .dyr files often contain models GradPower does not implement. analyze_dyr_coverage reports, record by record, which ones are native, unsupported or without a matching generator:\n\nusing GradPower\nex  = joinpath(pkgdir(GradPower), \"examples\")\nrep = analyze_dyr_coverage(joinpath(ex, \"IEEE39.raw\"), joinpath(ex, \"IEEE39_gov.dyr\"))\ncoverage_counts(rep)\nDict{Symbol, Int64} with 7 entries:\n  :native      => 20\n  :redirected  => 0\n  :unmatched   => 0\n  :active      => 20\n  :duplicate   => 0\n  :inactive    => 0\n  :unsupported => 0\n\nBy default unsupported records are skipped with a warning, and their machine keeps running without that controller. from_psse(raw, dyr; surrogates = true) instead maps common unsupported governors and exciters (GGOV1, HYGOV, IEEEG1, EXAC, ESST, ...) onto TGOV1/SEXS stand-ins. A surrogate lets a large case run end to end. It is not the original model, and results obtained with surrogates must not be presented as a validation of it.","title":"Checking what a .dyr file uses"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/models/#Bundled-cases","page":"Supported models and input files","text":"The examples/ folder contains small cases used throughout this documentation and the tests:\n\ncase buses machines .dyr variants\n2bus.raw 2 1 2bus.dyr, _IEESGO, _TGOV1, _SEXS, _ESDC1A, _IEEEST\nieee9_v33.raw (WSCC 9-bus) 9 3 ieee9bus.dyr, _gov, _SEXS, _TGOV1\nIEEE39.raw (New England) 39 10 IEEE39.dyr, IEEE39_gov.dyr\n\nLarger synthetic grids (ACTIVSg200 to ACTIVSg70k, from the Texas A&M electric-grid test-case repository) also work, but are not bundled.","title":"Bundled cases"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/recording/#Recording-and-early-stop","page":"Recording and early stop","text":"By default integrate! returns the full trajectory: every state at every step, in Float64. For large systems that is a lot of memory. ACTIVSg70k has 216,698 unknowns, so 10 s at 1/120 s is about 2 GB per simulation. The recording options store only what you need. They never change the computed solution.\n\noption effect\nsave_every = s store steps 1, 1+s, 1+2s, … (the initial state is always stored)\nsave_idxs = rows store only these rows of the state\nsave_eltype = Float32 store in single precision\nstep_callback = (k, t, z) -> … called with the full state after every step, stored or not\nstop_condition = (k, t, z) -> Bool end the simulation when it returns true\n\nusing GradPower, CairoMakie\nex = joinpath(pkgdir(GradPower), \"examples\")\nsys = from_psse(joinpath(ex, \"IEEE39.raw\"), joinpath(ex, \"IEEE39_gov.dyr\"))\nbuild_network!(sys); runpf!(sys)\ndp = DynamicProblem(sys); initialize_dynamics!(dp, sys)\nz0 = copy(dp.zvec)\nadd_event!(sys, ContingencyEvent(sys.busmap[16], 0.05, 0.2, 0.3))","title":"Recording and early stop"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/recording/#Named-results","page":"Recording and early stop","text":"The default return remains (times, trajectory). Opt into a SimulationResult with result=true on the CPU or GPU integrators:\n\nres = integrate!(dp, sys, 5.0; result=true)\nw = speed(res, 1)                                  # pu speed deviation, view\nw = state(res, :genrou, 1, :w)                      # same raw state\nvm = bus_voltage(res, 16; component=:magnitude)      # external bus 16, pu\nvr = bus_voltage(res, 16; component=:real)           # raw state view\n\nUse state(res, :genrou, bus, \"1\", :w) for explicit external bus and device identity. The short k form sorts recorded devices by (external bus, device ID), independently of solver layout. Controller state names retain their declared case (for example :PF0 for IEESGO). speed returns deviation from synchronous speed, not absolute speed or frequency in Hz. Voltage angle is in radians.\n\nres.channels describes every saved raw row, including device algebraic states and rectangular network voltages. state_channels(sys; save_idxs=rows) exposes the same schema without running a simulation. The schema snapshots initial online flags; applied scheduled events are in res.events, and limit events are in res.metadata.limit_events. Samples at event steps precede their algebraic re-solve. Missing states in selective recordings raise ArgumentError. Raw accessors return views; derived complex voltage, magnitude, and angle allocate.\n\nres.status is [:success] or [:terminated] for early stop; failed Newton solves still throw. res.metadata.final_time gives the actual stop time even when a downsampled output does not include that step. res.times always matches the stored columns.\n\nBatches keep one matrix per scenario in res.trajectories. Supply scenario_ids=[:base, :fault] and select with speed(res, 1; scenario=:fault). IDs default to 1:M. Result objects can still be unpacked as times, trajectories = res.","title":"Named results"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/recording/#Store-less","page":"Recording and early stop","text":"Keep only the bus voltages, every 6th step (20 samples per second), in Float32:\n\nnet  = sys.dynamic.diff_dim + sys.dynamic.alg_dim\nrows = collect(net + 1 : net + 2length(sys.buses))\n\ndp.zvec .= z0; t_full, full = integrate!(dp, sys, 5.0)\ndp.zvec .= z0; t, V = integrate!(dp, sys, 5.0; save_idxs = rows, save_every = 6,\n                                 save_eltype = Float32)\n(full = Base.summarysize(full), reduced = Base.summarysize(V),\n same_values = V == Float32.(full[rows, 1:6:end]))\n(full = 1144352, reduced = 31560, same_values = true)\n\nThe reduced output is exactly a slice of the full one.","title":"Store less"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/recording/#Compute-statistics-on-the-fly","page":"Recording and early stop","text":"step_callback sees every step at full resolution. It is the place to accumulate quantities without storing the trajectory: peaks, energies, the labels of a dataset. Here, the lowest voltage seen at each bus:\n\nvmin = fill(Inf, length(sys.buses))\ncb = (k, t, z) -> begin\n    for b in eachindex(vmin)\n        vmin[b] = min(vmin[b], hypot(z[net + 2b - 1], z[net + 2b]))\n    end\nend\ndp.zvec .= z0\nintegrate!(dp, sys, 5.0; save_idxs = Int[], step_callback = cb)   # stores nothing\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 300))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"PSS/E bus\", ylabel = \"min |V| during the run (pu)\",\n          xticks = 1:3:39)\nbarplot!(ax, [b.i for b in sys.buses], vmin)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Compute statistics on the fly"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/recording/#Stop-early","page":"Recording and early stop","text":"A fault cleared too late makes some machines lose synchronism: rotor angles separate without bound. There is no point simulating such a run to the end. stop_condition ends it as soon as the outcome is known. The output then holds the steps up to that point.\n\nempty!(sys.dynamic.events)\nadd_event!(sys, ContingencyEvent(sys.busmap[16], 0.001, 0.2, 0.8))   # 600 ms bolted-like fault\n\ndelta = [i + 1 for i in GradPower.gen_speeds(sys)]                  # rotor angle rows\nd0 = z0[delta]\nseparation(z) = (d = z[delta] .- d0; maximum(d) - minimum(d))      # relative to t = 0\n\ndp.zvec .= z0; tf, full = integrate!(dp, sys, 3.0)\ndp.zvec .= z0; ts, part = integrate!(dp, sys, 3.0;\n                    stop_condition = (k, t, z) -> separation(z) > deg2rad(360))\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 330))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"time (s)\", ylabel = \"rotor angle separation (deg)\")\nlines!(ax, tf, rad2deg.(separation.(eachcol(full))), color = :gray, label = \"full run\")\nlines!(ax, ts, rad2deg.(separation.(eachcol(part))), linewidth = 4,\n       label = \"stopped at $(round(ts[end], digits = 2)) s\")\nhlines!(ax, [360], color = :red, linestyle = :dash)\naxislegend(ax, position = :lt)\nempty!(sys.dynamic.events)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe separation is measured relative to the initial angles. Large systems can already span close to 200° at equilibrium, so the absolute spread is not a reliable instability test.","title":"Stop early"},{"category":"section","location":"guides/recording/#Batches","page":"Recording and early stop","text":"integrate_batched! takes the same save_* options, applied to every scenario. Its callback is step_callback(m, k, t, z), with the scenario index m first.","title":"Batches"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/state_vector/#Reading-the-results","page":"Reading the results","text":"integrate! returns (t, traj). Column j of traj is the complete state of the DAE at time t[j]:\n\nz = [ x  differential states   (machines, governors, exciters, ...)\n      y  algebraic states      (machine stator voltages/currents, ...)\n      v  bus voltages          (vr₁, vi₁, vr₂, vi₂, ...)  rectangular, pu ]\n\nEach device owns a contiguous slice of x and y. Here is how to find any quantity.\n\nusing GradPower, CairoMakie\nex = joinpath(pkgdir(GradPower), \"examples\")\nsys = from_psse(joinpath(ex, \"ieee9_v33.raw\"), joinpath(ex, \"ieee9bus_gov.dyr\"))\nbuild_network!(sys); runpf!(sys)\ndp = DynamicProblem(sys); initialize_dynamics!(dp, sys)\n(diff = sys.dynamic.diff_dim, alg = sys.dynamic.alg_dim, voltages = 2length(sys.buses))\n(diff = 33, alg = 15, voltages = 18)","title":"Reading the results"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/state_vector/#Devices-and-their-states","page":"Reading the results","text":"Every device lists its own states by name:\n\ndd = sys.dynamic.diff_dim\nfor d in sys.dynamic.devices\n    names = vcat(get_diff_names(d.dtype), get_alg_names(d.dtype))\n    isempty(names) && continue\n    println(rpad(string(nameof(typeof(d.dtype))), 7), \" bus \", d.dtype.bus,\n            \"  x[\", d.diff_ptr, \":\", d.diff_ptr + d.dtype.diff_size - 1, \"] \",\n            get_diff_names(d.dtype), \"  y \", get_alg_names(d.dtype))\nend\nGenrou  bus 1  x[1:6] [\"e_qp\", \"e_dp\", \"phi_1d\", \"phi_2q\", \"w\", \"delta\"]  y [\"v_q\", \"v_d\", \"i_q\", \"i_d\"]\nGenrou  bus 2  x[17:22] [\"e_qp\", \"e_dp\", \"phi_1d\", \"phi_2q\", \"w\", \"delta\"]  y [\"v_q\", \"v_d\", \"i_q\", \"i_d\"]\nGenrou  bus 3  x[33:38] [\"e_qp\", \"e_dp\", \"phi_1d\", \"phi_2q\", \"w\", \"delta\"]  y [\"v_q\", \"v_d\", \"i_q\", \"i_d\"]\nIEESGO  bus 1  x[11:15] [\"PF0\", \"PLL\", \"TP1\", \"TP2\", \"TP3\"]  y [\"p_m\"]\nIEESGO  bus 2  x[27:31] [\"PF0\", \"PLL\", \"TP1\", \"TP2\", \"TP3\"]  y [\"p_m\"]\nIEESGO  bus 3  x[43:47] [\"PF0\", \"PLL\", \"TP1\", \"TP2\", \"TP3\"]  y [\"p_m\"]\n\nSo for a GENROU machine d, rotor speed and angle are\n\nw     = traj[d.diff_ptr + 4, :]     # speed deviation (pu)\ndelta = traj[d.diff_ptr + 5, :]     # rotor angle (rad)\n\nand its algebraic states (e.g. stator currents i_q, i_d) are at traj[dd + d.alg_ptr + k, :]. GradPower.gen_speeds(sys) returns the speed rows of all machines.","title":"Devices and their states"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/state_vector/#Bus-voltages","page":"Reading the results","text":"Voltages are the last 2 * nbus rows, interleaved as real/imaginary parts:\n\nadd_event!(sys, ContingencyEvent(sys.busmap[5], 0.05, 0.1, 0.25))\nt, traj = integrate!(dp, sys, 3.0)\n\nnet = sys.dynamic.diff_dim + sys.dynamic.alg_dim\nV(b) = complex.(traj[net + 2b - 1, :], traj[net + 2b, :])\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 320))\nax1 = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"time (s)\", ylabel = \"|V| (pu)\", title = \"magnitude\")\nax2 = Axis(fig[1, 2], xlabel = \"time (s)\", ylabel = \"angle (deg)\", title = \"angle\")\nfor b in eachindex(sys.buses)\n    lines!(ax1, t, abs.(V(b)))\n    lines!(ax2, t, rad2deg.(angle.(V(b))))\nend\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Bus voltages"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/state_vector/#Power-from-a-machine","page":"Reading the results","text":"Quantities that are not states can be computed from them. For example, the electrical power of each GENROU machine P = vr·ir + vi·ii (here from the stator voltages and currents in the machine's dq frame):\n\nfig = Figure(size = (800, 300))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1], xlabel = \"time (s)\", ylabel = \"P electrical (pu, machine base)\")\nfor d in sys.dynamic.devices\n    d.dtype isa GradPower.Genrou || continue\n    a = dd + d.alg_ptr                         # [v_q, v_d, i_q, i_d]\n    P = traj[a, :] .* traj[a + 2, :] .+ traj[a + 1, :] .* traj[a + 3, :]\n    lines!(ax, t, P, label = \"gen at bus $(d.dtype.bus)\")\nend\naxislegend(ax, position = :rb)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Power from a machine"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/state_vector/#Parameters","page":"Reading the results","text":"The parameters of every device live in dp.pvec (the kernels read them from there). They are listed by get_param_names:\n\ng = sys.dynamic.devices[findfirst(d -> d.dtype isa GradPower.Genrou, sys.dynamic.devices)]\ncollect(zip(get_param_names(g.dtype), dp.pvec[g.par_ptr .+ (0:g.dtype.par_size-1)]))\n14-element Vector{Tuple{String, Float64}}:\n (\"x_d\", 0.741)\n (\"x_q\", 0.7093076923076923)\n (\"x_dp\", 0.1466153846153846)\n (\"x_qp\", 0.21034615384615385)\n (\"x_ddp\", 0.1111153846153846)\n (\"xl\", 0.09396153846153844)\n (\"H\", 11.206)\n (\"D\", 0.026000000000000002)\n (\"T_d0p\", 7.729)\n (\"T_q0p\", 0.859)\n (\"T_d0dp\", 0.047)\n (\"T_q0dp\", 0.068)\n (\"S1\", 0.115)\n (\"S2\", 0.627)\n\nChanging dp.pvec before integrate! changes the model. For example, dp.pvec[g.par_ptr + 6] *= 2 doubles that machine's inertia H. Scenario studies use this through integrate_batched!.","title":"Parameters"}]
}
